1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,000
They process billions of transactions a year involving trillions of dollars.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:11,000
The money flows through both huge repositories and tiny computers.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
It is their job to make sure the wealth of the world is absolutely secure.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, Banks on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
By their very nature and imposing design, banks are mighty financial fortresses that

6
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:47,160
exude an aura of wealth and opulence.

7
00:00:47,161 --> 00:00:53,080
The message of architecture and bank history is a very interesting one.

8
00:00:53,081 --> 00:01:00,740
A bank building radiating the message of solidity, stability, trustworthiness.

9
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:08,200
A bank's powerful structure offers the implicit promise that your money is safe inside these walls.

10
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:16,600
Throughout history, banks have maybe given a little more attention than most businesses to their appearance.

11
00:01:16,601 --> 00:01:22,240
big skyscrapers showing that there's a substantial resources there are part of

12
00:01:22,241 --> 00:01:26,380
the mystique of banking to give people that confidence.

13
00:01:26,381 --> 00:01:33,340
The bank is more than a building. A bank is something very special. A bank is

14
00:01:33,341 --> 00:01:39,040
trust, faith that people have in the bankers, in what they do.

15
00:01:39,041 --> 00:01:43,060
It is that representation of trust that is at the very core of a bank's

16
00:01:43,061 --> 00:01:43,829
existence.

17
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:50,060
Without it, a depositor loses faith and a bank reneges on its fundamental pledge.

18
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:57,680
Within the past decade, modern technology has launched a revolution that has dramatically

19
00:01:57,681 --> 00:02:01,644
altered the way banks and their customers do business.

20
00:02:01,645 --> 00:02:03,920
A new era of electronic banking

21
00:02:03,921 --> 00:02:08,780
has moved to the Internet, and soon it may even be possible to do your banking in the

22
00:02:08,781 --> 00:02:12,100
kitchen using a microwave oven.

23
00:02:12,101 --> 00:02:16,740
We realize the Internet is a powerful new tool that's going to change financial services

24
00:02:16,741 --> 00:02:17,960
and the way they're delivered.

25
00:02:17,961 --> 00:02:21,600
Today there are less than 10 million consumers doing online banking and that'll be over

26
00:02:21,601 --> 00:02:26,840
a hundred million in the near future.

27
00:02:26,841 --> 00:02:31,740
From millionaires to average depositors, security has always been the most important thing a

28
00:02:31,741 --> 00:02:34,460
bank has to offer.

29
00:02:34,461 --> 00:02:39,500
Nowhere else is that security more apparent than inside a vault.

30
00:02:39,501 --> 00:02:42,460
They want to see a vault door.

31
00:02:42,461 --> 00:02:45,880
Little children and old men, they want to see them.

32
00:02:45,881 --> 00:02:49,400
Little old ladies, it's my dollar in there.

33
00:02:49,401 --> 00:02:51,557
At the historic J.P.

34
00:02:51,558 --> 00:02:59,320
Morgan building on Wall Street, this 52-ton vault protected the economic

35
00:02:59,321 --> 00:03:05,920
empires of America's business elite during the early part of the 20th century.

36
00:03:05,921 --> 00:03:10,840
The vault stood in silent defiance to anyone who would dare even think of crossing its

37
00:03:10,841 --> 00:03:14,980
threshold without proper authorization.

38
00:03:14,981 --> 00:03:20,480
Morgan's gargantuan vault was the heart of the bank itself, literally the cornerstone

39
00:03:20,481 --> 00:03:23,100
of its success.

40
00:03:23,101 --> 00:03:25,540
It's the backbone, the spine of it all.

41
00:03:25,541 --> 00:03:29,660
That's what people see, that's what they recognize, and that's what they feel comfortable with.

42
00:03:29,661 --> 00:03:33,100
They feel that the bigger their vault is, the more successful their bank is.

43
00:03:33,101 --> 00:03:39,680
A modern bank vault is a six-sided box constructed of different layers of various materials.

44
00:03:39,681 --> 00:03:45,780
The innermost layer is a thick steel lining that makes up the floor, walls, and ceiling.

45
00:03:45,781 --> 00:03:51,460
A concrete casing surrounds the steel cage.

46
00:03:51,461 --> 00:03:59,240
It all encased by a web-like system of steel reinforcing rods inserted both in and around the concrete shell for additional strength.

47
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:06,700
The most visible and impressive feature of a bank vault has always been its door.

48
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:11,140
The banker, he wanted to make sure that he was protecting the assets of his clients,

49
00:04:11,141 --> 00:04:16,140
and so bigger was better as far as thick walls with a big massive vault door on it.

50
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:19,800
The vault door is a mountain of metal.

51
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:24,840
Its hinges must be able to support a weight of as much as 20 tons,

52
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:28,500
but be able to swing open smoothly and efficiently.

53
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:33,560
Advancements in technology enabled the construction of bank vaults

54
00:04:33,561 --> 00:04:37,020
to change considerably over the latter part of the 20th century.

55
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:43,900
In the 20s, bank vaults were 27 to 30 inches of concrete.

56
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:47,640
Now they are making them down to 12 inches of concrete.

57
00:04:47,641 --> 00:04:52,440
The concrete is 8 to 12 times stronger than it was in the 1920s.

58
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:59,340
The steel materials that are going into the safes are also 10 to 12 times stronger than they were in the 1920s.

59
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:07,720
Vault doors face a trial by fire at the Underwriters Laboratory in Illinois.

60
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:13,000
Technicians attack vault doors to determine how reliable they really are.

61
00:05:13,001 --> 00:05:27,001
The main concern of a banker would be to put a product in there that has been tested by certified trained professionals to emulate what a high-skilled burglar would

62
00:05:27,035 --> 00:05:30,800
bring in to try to penetrate that structure.

63
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:37,700
The longer it takes to successfully assault a vault, the higher its security rating.

64
00:05:37,701 --> 00:05:46,540
The UL rating system for safes starts at a five-minute container and progresses through

65
00:05:46,541 --> 00:05:48,920
15 minutes, 30 minutes, and one hour.

66
00:05:48,921 --> 00:05:54,640
At the highest end, which would be one hour and explosion resistant, you would find a

67
00:05:54,641 --> 00:05:58,060
jewelry safe, which could contain several million dollars worth of jewelry.

68
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:04,660
Vault construction also incorporates a variety of sophisticated alarm systems.

69
00:06:04,661 --> 00:06:10,140
A sound accumulator is a device that is actually a microphone that's placed inside the vault,

70
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:16,100
and it listens to and for sounds, like somebody pounding on the side of a vault,

71
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:21,360
and it accumulates it and keeps track of it, and after a certain amount of time or a certain level is reached,

72
00:06:21,780 --> 00:06:23,280
it then trips the alarm.

73
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:32,340
Bank vaults include special openings for other electrical systems that support interior lights and conduits for telephone lines.

74
00:06:32,341 --> 00:06:39,000
It is critical that vaults also have openings to accommodate air intake and exhaust conduits.

75
00:06:40,140 --> 00:06:45,940
The earlier ones didn't have it, the newer ones do, but the banks would always put in what we call a vault ventilator system.

76
00:06:46,180 --> 00:06:53,620
It's in case you were locked into a vault in a hostage situation or just hurt it in there after a robbery or whatever,

77
00:06:54,160 --> 00:07:00,860
the employees or the people inside that vault would have the ability to turn a fan on that would suck air in from the outside.

78
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:10,220
In addition to guarding the bank's separate cash containers, a typical vault also houses a gleaming array of safe deposit boxes.

79
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:14,080
They are actually a safe within a safe.

80
00:07:14,860 --> 00:07:18,560
But surprisingly, they may not be quite as sturdy as you think.

81
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:24,940
It's a casing, which is a five-sided box that was a quarter-inch thick solid steel.

82
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:30,800
And then on there, some brass hinges were put on there with a half-inch thick polished steel door.

83
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:35,900
The boxes have basically gotten lighter because your casings are thinner now.

84
00:07:36,060 --> 00:07:39,320
Some are just hollow doors with a lock on them.

85
00:07:39,620 --> 00:07:44,620
The banking industry said, hey, you know, I'm spending X amount of dollars to build this massive concrete vault

86
00:07:44,621 --> 00:07:46,920
and putting this expensive vault door on it.

87
00:07:47,060 --> 00:07:50,200
Give me something that's cheaper to lock up inside of my room here.

88
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:56,600
A safe deposit box has two keys, one for the customer, one for the bank.

89
00:07:57,340 --> 00:08:00,700
The box can't be opened without two keys at the same time.

90
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:05,680
Everything in a bank for auditing purposes is under what we call dual custody.

91
00:08:05,681 --> 00:08:12,800
Whether it be a vault door, whether it be a night depository receiving chest, a safe deposit box, a cash safe, a teller's locker,

92
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:18,300
it's going to take more than one person to get access to open that door to get whatever the contents are out.

93
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:23,800
Simple combination locks opened early bank vaults and safes.

94
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:29,500
But this kind of lock was attractive to criminals who could open them with relative ease.

95
00:08:29,501 --> 00:08:36,600
It also created the possibility that a bank manager could be kidnapped and forced to open his own vault.

96
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:41,080
Time locks were developed as a way to prevent that.

97
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:46,200
The first time lock in a bank vault was installed in 1874.

98
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:53,680
A time clock is a timing mechanism with appropriate linkage internally

99
00:08:53,681 --> 00:08:58,380
that has to coordinate with the linkage on the combinations.

100
00:08:59,280 --> 00:09:02,720
So even though you have, say, the combination properly,

101
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:06,400
if there's still time on the time clocks when you wind them,

102
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:08,540
the combinations aren't going to work.

103
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:14,480
As many as four time locks were installed on bank vaults as a fail-safe system.

104
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:18,620
If one timer stops working, the others continue to operate.

105
00:09:19,780 --> 00:09:24,240
Standard time locks were clocks that could be wound for a period of up to 96 hours,

106
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:26,140
or four full days.

107
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:33,160
Some modern vaults use digital time lock technology that allows for an infinite time setting, if so desired.

108
00:09:33,860 --> 00:09:37,640
If you try to run your combinations before those time locks have wound down,

109
00:09:37,641 --> 00:09:40,980
you put the correct combination on the combination locks,

110
00:09:41,180 --> 00:09:45,980
but you're still not able to re-tack the locking bolts or the locking bar of that safe or vault door.

111
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:53,060
As imposing as a bank vault is, with its massive steel and sophisticated locks,

112
00:09:53,061 --> 00:09:56,360
banks are still a lucrative target for criminals.

113
00:09:57,360 --> 00:10:02,240
In 1986, burglars tunneled into a first interstate bank vault in Los Angeles.

114
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:06,720
They stole $2 million worth of cash, jewels, and rare coins.

115
00:10:07,460 --> 00:10:10,060
The burglars and their booty have never been found.

116
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:13,740
Banks will continue on Modern Marvels.

117
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:20,740
We now return to Banks on Modern Marvels.

118
00:10:20,741 --> 00:10:29,740
Basic forms of deposit and lending can be traced back to the temples and royal treasuries of Mesopotamia in the 3rd century BC.

119
00:10:29,741 --> 00:10:34,740
The ancient Greeks developed strongholds which were used for the safe storage of valuables.

120
00:10:34,741 --> 00:10:41,740
The modern concept of a banking system of deposits, withdrawals and loans began in Italy during the Middle Ages.

121
00:10:41,741 --> 00:10:46,740
The word bank itself comes from an Italian word called banco, which means bench.

122
00:10:46,741 --> 00:10:47,441
bench.

123
00:10:47,442 --> 00:10:57,060
And the first bankers, nearly a thousand years ago, would go into the piazzas in Venice

124
00:10:57,061 --> 00:11:01,217
and Florence and put down their bench, pull out their account book.

125
00:11:01,218 --> 00:11:03,140
Somebody would give them money,

126
00:11:03,260 --> 00:11:04,407
they would get a credit.

127
00:11:04,408 --> 00:11:07,800
Somebody would borrow money, they would get a debit or took out a loan.

128
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:14,860
During the 17th century, goldsmiths in England provided a more contemporary model for modern

129
00:11:14,861 --> 00:11:15,561
banking.

130
00:11:15,562 --> 00:11:19,260
Goldsmiths were some of the first bankers because they handled a lot of gold,

131
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:24,060
and people learned that they could deposit their gold safely with them and use their

132
00:11:24,061 --> 00:11:26,197
accounts with those bankers.

133
00:11:26,198 --> 00:11:31,080
The goldsmiths had large amounts of gold on deposit from wealthy

134
00:11:31,081 --> 00:11:37,065
clients, which often sat untouched for many years before it was used or returned.

135
00:11:37,066 --> 00:11:38,100
The goldsmiths

136
00:11:38,101 --> 00:11:43,840
began to loan some of that gold out to others in exchange for a promissory note and the payment of

137
00:11:43,841 --> 00:11:45,071
interest charge.

138
00:11:45,072 --> 00:11:50,840
In time, paper certificates redeemable in gold coin were circulated instead

139
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:51,987
of gold itself.

140
00:11:51,988 --> 00:11:57,520
When the British Empire reached across the Atlantic Ocean to colonize North

141
00:11:57,521 --> 00:12:03,500
America, British banking houses stood at one end of a long chain of global credit that

142
00:12:03,501 --> 00:12:06,900
stretched all the way to the new American frontier.

143
00:12:06,901 --> 00:12:11,287
Banking only really came to the United States after the American Revolution.

144
00:12:11,288 --> 00:12:11,988
Before that,

145
00:12:11,989 --> 00:12:15,127
was effectively prohibited by British regulations.

146
00:12:15,128 --> 00:12:17,960
And any banking that would have existed prior

147
00:12:17,961 --> 00:12:22,840
to that would have been banking done by merchants, even dry goods merchants, providing various

148
00:12:22,841 --> 00:12:28,760
forms of credit to their customers, or perhaps various bills, short-term forms of lending.

149
00:12:28,940 --> 00:12:31,260
But nothing we would formally consider as a bank.

150
00:12:32,980 --> 00:12:38,220
It was a confusing economic period because so many different kinds of currency were already

151
00:12:38,221 --> 00:12:39,315
in circulation.

152
00:12:39,316 --> 00:12:44,860
In addition to British shillings and pounds, colonists were also using coins

153
00:12:44,861 --> 00:12:46,264
from Spain and France.

154
00:12:46,265 --> 00:12:51,240
Individual states also began issuing their own money of various denominations.

155
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:57,243
The first bank formed in the United States was formed by Congress in 1781.

156
00:12:57,244 --> 00:12:58,720
It didn't open until

157
00:12:58,721 --> 00:12:59,421
1782.

158
00:12:59,422 --> 00:13:04,620
It was a part of the effort to straighten out the chaotic finances of the revolution.

159
00:13:04,621 --> 00:13:05,956
That was the very first bank.

160
00:13:05,957 --> 00:13:08,720
It was in Philadelphia and called the Bank of North America.

161
00:13:10,380 --> 00:13:16,340
One of its main supporters was Alexander Hamilton, who would become the first secretary of the Treasury.

162
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:24,680
But Hamilton had bitter disagreements with other founding fathers over both the necessity and the legality of a federal bank.

163
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:27,940
Of course, his great antagonist was Thomas Jefferson.

164
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:30,710
Jefferson wanted to keep the government small.

165
00:13:30,711 --> 00:13:32,720
He wanted us to remain a nation of farmers.

166
00:13:32,721 --> 00:13:36,280
In Hamilton's view, banks were a key component of development.

167
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:40,920
Jefferson's view was that, well, you know, the Constitution didn't mention banks.

168
00:13:41,060 --> 00:13:42,180
Maybe we didn't really need them.

169
00:13:43,860 --> 00:13:46,840
While the new nation and its banks began to flourish,

170
00:13:47,480 --> 00:13:51,140
the debate over the role of banks themselves would rage for decades.

171
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:56,600
By the 1800s, there were almost 700 private banks in the United States.

172
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:01,300
Early banks were not the huge institutions that one pictures today.

173
00:14:01,301 --> 00:14:06,400
Many banks had their origins in merchants' business,

174
00:14:06,700 --> 00:14:11,940
and therefore the banking business would have been done just on the floor of, say, the dry goods store.

175
00:14:12,200 --> 00:14:16,640
And slowly over time, the merchant might have moved into specializing in banking.

176
00:14:18,580 --> 00:14:22,260
Each transaction was painstakingly recorded in longhand.

177
00:14:22,880 --> 00:14:27,200
Each deposit, each withdrawal, every loan recorded down to the cent.

178
00:14:27,201 --> 00:14:34,700
In 1848, a discovery at Sutter's Mill near Sacramento, California, changed the economics

179
00:14:34,701 --> 00:14:37,460
of the developing nation practically overnight.

180
00:14:37,461 --> 00:14:43,500
The history of banking in the western United States is the history of the discovery of

181
00:14:43,501 --> 00:14:51,158
gold. Gold in the 19th century is the basis of the U.S. monetary system. All U.S.

182
00:14:51,159 --> 00:14:51,859
dollars

183
00:14:51,860 --> 00:14:54,660
are U.S. gold dollars in the form of gold coins.

184
00:14:55,720 --> 00:15:00,225
When gold is discovered, it creates the greatest migration of people in the U.S.

185
00:15:00,226 --> 00:15:02,140
history from one place to another.

186
00:15:03,440 --> 00:15:07,820
More than 200,000 people from all over the world headed to California

187
00:15:07,821 --> 00:15:10,980
in the three years following the discovery of gold.

188
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:17,180
The owners of a thriving Eastern Express Service company were uniquely poised

189
00:15:17,181 --> 00:15:20,880
to handle the increased demands on finance and transportation systems

190
00:15:20,881 --> 00:15:24,740
that were spurred by the rapid movement of Americans headed west.

191
00:15:27,160 --> 00:15:33,080
Henry Wells and William Fargo developed one of the first great business concepts in American history.

192
00:15:33,720 --> 00:15:39,300
The concept that Wells and Fargo developed was combining the transportation and the movement

193
00:15:39,301 --> 00:15:44,300
and the security of money with then banking in the form of the extension of credit.

194
00:15:45,660 --> 00:15:49,140
Miners needed a place to exchange their gold nuggets for currency.

195
00:15:49,940 --> 00:15:53,240
Banks needed a way to deal with the sudden influx of the precious metal.

196
00:15:53,520 --> 00:16:01,820
A miner in a gold field would bring his raw gold in the form of dust or nuggets into a Wells Fargo office.

197
00:16:02,460 --> 00:16:05,240
The raw gold was then typically put on a stagecoach,

198
00:16:06,040 --> 00:16:10,100
which then took off to meet either a railroad or a riverboat

199
00:16:10,101 --> 00:16:16,200
that would take it to a place like San Francisco or Carson City, Nevada or Denver, Colorado,

200
00:16:16,201 --> 00:16:19,360
where it would be transformed into gold coin.

201
00:16:21,260 --> 00:16:26,920
Wells Fargo offices of every variety sprang up throughout the West to cash in on the new economy.

202
00:16:29,060 --> 00:16:34,020
The gold rush had an enormous impact on the rapid expansion of banks in the United States.

203
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:39,920
By the early 1850s, almost 1,600 banks existed throughout the country.

204
00:16:40,880 --> 00:16:47,700
Less than a decade later, another momentous event in American history would change American banks forever.

205
00:16:51,660 --> 00:16:57,640
The Civil War not only divided the United States, it came dangerously close to bankrupting the Republic.

206
00:16:58,580 --> 00:17:07,180
By 1860, there was still no national currency, and the Civil War made a chaotic condition even worse by creating an avalanche of competing currencies.

207
00:17:08,180 --> 00:17:10,180
Confederate states printed their own bills.

208
00:17:10,920 --> 00:17:12,920
Banks in Union states did the same.

209
00:17:13,620 --> 00:17:17,600
Before the Civil War, all the banks of the country issued their own bank notes.

210
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:20,880
Each one issued several denominations of bank notes,

211
00:17:20,960 --> 00:17:23,860
a 1, a 5, a 10, a 20, maybe a 50.

212
00:17:24,620 --> 00:17:28,980
So take, say, five different denominations of notes and 1,600 banks,

213
00:17:28,981 --> 00:17:34,920
you had 8,000 different appearing pieces of paper all representing United States dollars.

214
00:17:34,921 --> 00:17:42,120
At the end of the Civil War, the federal government mandated a single currency for the reunited nation.

215
00:17:42,900 --> 00:17:49,840
It enforced that order by imposing a 10% tax on earlier currency, essentially making it worth that much less.

216
00:17:50,660 --> 00:17:58,200
In 1866, at the end of the Civil War, the federal government passed a law saying state banks could no longer issue these notes.

217
00:17:58,720 --> 00:18:01,140
And so we got federally backed currency.

218
00:18:01,141 --> 00:18:07,140
Out West, the gold rush had ended and a silver boom had begun in Nevada.

219
00:18:07,141 --> 00:18:13,220
But the entire banking structure of the western United States was about to be tested by one

220
00:18:13,221 --> 00:18:17,900
of the most devastating natural disasters of all time.

221
00:18:17,901 --> 00:18:23,200
We now return to Banks on Modern Marvels.

222
00:18:23,201 --> 00:18:27,760
At the beginning of the 20th century, the city of San Francisco had begun to come of

223
00:18:27,761 --> 00:18:29,140
age.

224
00:18:29,141 --> 00:18:32,417
gold rush era had given it a substantial population.

225
00:18:32,418 --> 00:18:35,000
By this time, Wells Fargo Bank had become

226
00:18:35,001 --> 00:18:38,482
deeply entrenched in the economic infrastructure.

227
00:18:38,483 --> 00:18:41,680
Then, everything literally changed overnight.

228
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:51,380
In April 1906, a great earthquake leveled San Francisco, but the earthquake was not the worst

229
00:18:51,381 --> 00:18:52,081
of it.

230
00:18:52,082 --> 00:18:58,960
The firestorm that followed the earthquake destroyed all of the remaining infrastructure of

231
00:18:58,961 --> 00:19:01,460
virtually the entire city.

232
00:19:01,461 --> 00:19:04,980
The banks in particular were in a difficult position

233
00:19:04,981 --> 00:19:07,700
because their vaults were so hot

234
00:19:07,701 --> 00:19:10,500
as a result of the ensuing firestorm

235
00:19:10,501 --> 00:19:14,100
that it was days before they could enter the vaults

236
00:19:14,101 --> 00:19:15,940
and see whether or not, in fact,

237
00:19:15,941 --> 00:19:17,880
they were still in business.

238
00:19:17,881 --> 00:19:21,300
The president of the bank sent a famous telegram

239
00:19:21,301 --> 00:19:23,680
to all depositors and correspondents.

240
00:19:24,640 --> 00:19:28,020
Building destroyed, vaults intact,

241
00:19:28,021 --> 00:19:31,020
credit unaffected.

242
00:19:31,021 --> 00:19:34,320
Eventually he discovered that the contents of the vault had indeed

243
00:19:34,321 --> 00:19:36,980
survived intact.

244
00:19:36,981 --> 00:19:40,980
The calamity was also a trial by fire for another San Francisco financial

245
00:19:40,981 --> 00:19:42,200
institution,

246
00:19:42,201 --> 00:19:43,840
the Bank of Italy.

247
00:19:43,841 --> 00:19:47,820
The actions of its founder, AP Giannini, following the earthquake

248
00:19:47,821 --> 00:19:50,860
are still the stuff of legend.

249
00:19:50,861 --> 00:19:55,280
After the earthquake struck, AP made his way back up to the city

250
00:19:55,281 --> 00:19:57,460
and found the city in ruins.

251
00:19:57,860 --> 00:20:01,820
And the fire was starting to spread down in the financial district.

252
00:20:02,240 --> 00:20:06,160
Well, he and some bank officers decided to take what they could,

253
00:20:06,260 --> 00:20:09,340
the gold and the cash and the books, out of the bank.

254
00:20:09,440 --> 00:20:13,200
They put it in a wagon and covered it over with some orange crates,

255
00:20:13,600 --> 00:20:14,880
disguised it from looters,

256
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,960
and took it down through the crowd streaming out of the city.

257
00:20:17,961 --> 00:20:20,140
And when he decided to take that money out of that bank,

258
00:20:20,300 --> 00:20:22,840
put it into that wagon and get it out of town, he did it quickly.

259
00:20:22,841 --> 00:20:28,160
Many bankers wanted to stay closed for up to six months.

260
00:20:28,580 --> 00:20:31,000
They weren't sure the city was going to survive.

261
00:20:32,140 --> 00:20:33,920
A.P. said, that's wrong.

262
00:20:34,620 --> 00:20:36,300
He said, people need help now.

263
00:20:36,680 --> 00:20:39,680
If you don't start lending money, there will not be a San Francisco.

264
00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:43,480
He said, tomorrow morning, I'm going to put a desk out on Washington Street Wharf

265
00:20:43,481 --> 00:20:47,420
and advise all you bankers to beg, borrow, or steal a desk and follow my example.

266
00:20:47,940 --> 00:20:50,440
And he began making loans to rebuild the city.

267
00:20:50,441 --> 00:20:55,700
Giannini had originally opened the Bank of Italy in 1904.

268
00:20:56,580 --> 00:21:01,840
By 1918, he had expanded to 24 branches in 18 California cities.

269
00:21:02,920 --> 00:21:07,120
Ten years later, there were 292 branches throughout the state.

270
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:12,240
The creation of a vast network of bank branches will be Giannini's legacy.

271
00:21:13,020 --> 00:21:16,760
He saw the potential of a thing called branch banking in America

272
00:21:16,761 --> 00:21:19,420
when no one had ever applied that concept before.

273
00:21:19,421 --> 00:21:22,800
There were banks that had small branches and they were separate offices,

274
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:26,900
but what AP's vision was was to create a statewide network of branches.

275
00:21:28,820 --> 00:21:33,760
Bankers in other cities did not exactly welcome Giannini into their territory with open arms.

276
00:21:34,520 --> 00:21:37,660
They saw the spread of his banking empire as a real threat.

277
00:21:38,720 --> 00:21:41,220
Other bankers in Los Angeles didn't want him there.

278
00:21:41,540 --> 00:21:43,880
The L.A. bankers took out ads that said,

279
00:21:44,340 --> 00:21:46,220
California does not need another Mussolini.

280
00:21:46,221 --> 00:21:49,820
That did not stop Giannini.

281
00:21:50,360 --> 00:21:53,140
His associates said the snub served to inspire him.

282
00:21:53,760 --> 00:21:57,820
He bought a little bank in Los Angeles that had been founded in 1923

283
00:21:57,821 --> 00:21:59,940
called the Bank of America of Los Angeles.

284
00:22:00,820 --> 00:22:03,380
He held on to that name until 1930

285
00:22:03,381 --> 00:22:06,820
when he changed the name of all of his banks and banking systems

286
00:22:06,821 --> 00:22:10,300
that he had put together by that time into Bank of America.

287
00:22:11,760 --> 00:22:16,120
Giannini's vision eventually grew into the largest banking firm in the United States.

288
00:22:16,220 --> 00:22:25,620
Giannini's revolution in banking occurred at the same time as an American social revolution

289
00:22:25,621 --> 00:22:28,540
that was marked by the women's suffrage movement.

290
00:22:31,540 --> 00:22:34,860
But years before women were finally given the right to vote,

291
00:22:35,440 --> 00:22:39,020
they were actively solicited by banks as important customers,

292
00:22:39,021 --> 00:22:43,160
and they were enticed by promises of special treatment.

293
00:22:43,161 --> 00:22:52,520
Banks recognized women were responsible for managing household accounts, and they set up

294
00:22:52,521 --> 00:22:57,440
special areas in the bank for women customers.

295
00:22:57,441 --> 00:23:01,780
The Bank of New York was one of the first financial institutions to offer women a different

296
00:23:01,781 --> 00:23:05,960
banking experience from that of its male customers.

297
00:23:05,961 --> 00:23:08,640
The women's room in the bank was usually sectioned off.

298
00:23:08,641 --> 00:23:09,640
It was private.

299
00:23:09,641 --> 00:23:11,200
It was a little bit nicer.

300
00:23:11,201 --> 00:23:17,520
Lots of times there were a sofa perhaps or some chairs, a little sitting area, nicer plants,

301
00:23:17,740 --> 00:23:21,380
really more decorative, almost to feel like a parlor or a living room

302
00:23:21,381 --> 00:23:24,300
to make the women feel at ease with their financial transactions.

303
00:23:24,301 --> 00:23:28,820
It was really a marketing angle to attract women customers

304
00:23:28,821 --> 00:23:31,600
and make women feel more welcome in the bank

305
00:23:31,601 --> 00:23:34,560
and make their business a part of the bank's business.

306
00:23:36,220 --> 00:23:39,880
The bank's challenge with women was the same as it was with men,

307
00:23:40,600 --> 00:23:42,580
convincing them that their money was safe.

308
00:23:43,340 --> 00:23:46,920
One female customer who wasn't totally convinced was Hedy Green.

309
00:23:47,580 --> 00:23:50,260
She had inherited a fortune of millions of dollars

310
00:23:50,261 --> 00:23:53,980
and made millions more on her own by investing in the stock market.

311
00:23:54,640 --> 00:23:56,760
On Friday afternoon, she'd go into the bank.

312
00:23:57,300 --> 00:23:59,000
They would lead Hedy down into the vault.

313
00:23:59,160 --> 00:24:00,900
She would withdraw all of her money,

314
00:24:01,900 --> 00:24:03,720
count it, fill out a deposit ticket,

315
00:24:03,721 --> 00:24:06,020
and deposit all of her money back in the bank.

316
00:24:06,380 --> 00:24:07,720
I don't think she trusted the bank.

317
00:24:07,721 --> 00:24:17,080
It was a common theme of the era, brought on by hundreds of bank failures between 1870 and the early 1900s.

318
00:24:17,460 --> 00:24:22,220
The Federal Reserve System was established in 1913 to help change that trend.

319
00:24:22,740 --> 00:24:24,817
People lost confidence in their banks.

320
00:24:24,818 --> 00:24:27,440
Banks depended upon reserves of gold and silver.

321
00:24:27,900 --> 00:24:30,600
They didn't have a central bank backing up the transactions.

322
00:24:30,601 --> 00:24:34,200
and when people lost confidence or got scared,

323
00:24:34,720 --> 00:24:37,940
they'd run to the bank and they would take out their money

324
00:24:37,941 --> 00:24:40,840
and they'd cause a mini crisis in that region.

325
00:24:41,080 --> 00:24:43,000
That was a terrible situation.

326
00:24:44,380 --> 00:24:46,900
The Great Depression of the 1930s

327
00:24:46,901 --> 00:24:49,780
would pose the most monumental challenge to American banks

328
00:24:49,781 --> 00:24:51,220
and the country as a whole.

329
00:24:52,660 --> 00:24:57,060
Thousands of investors were wiped out in the stock market crash of 1929.

330
00:24:57,820 --> 00:25:00,420
But many banks had also made speculative investments

331
00:25:00,421 --> 00:25:01,333
on Wall Street.

332
00:25:01,334 --> 00:25:06,140
When the bottom dropped out of the stock market, it sent a shockwave of failure

333
00:25:06,141 --> 00:25:09,171
and despair through the American banking industry.

334
00:25:09,172 --> 00:25:12,020
The fact that you had 25,000 banks all over the

335
00:25:12,021 --> 00:25:17,580
United States meant you had 25,000 small institutions. And in banking, what's very

336
00:25:17,581 --> 00:25:21,840
important is to be diversified, be diversified in your depositors, diversified in your loans.

337
00:25:21,841 --> 00:25:24,305
These banks tend to be very specialized.

338
00:25:24,306 --> 00:25:27,140
And because they were small and undiversified,

339
00:25:27,141 --> 00:25:29,420
they were very susceptible to economic shocks.

340
00:25:30,260 --> 00:25:32,400
The Great Depression, of course, was a huge shock.

341
00:25:32,860 --> 00:25:35,700
There were massive bank failures across the United States.

342
00:25:36,160 --> 00:25:40,820
You went from having perhaps around 25,000 banks in the late 1920s

343
00:25:40,821 --> 00:25:43,960
to about 14,000 banks around 1933.

344
00:25:45,880 --> 00:25:50,740
The failure of so many banks combined with a nationwide loss of consumer confidence

345
00:25:50,741 --> 00:25:53,800
prompted people to stop spending what money they had.

346
00:25:54,780 --> 00:26:00,000
That loss of demand led to a dramatic drop-off in the country's production of consumer goods,

347
00:26:00,460 --> 00:26:02,220
making things even worse.

348
00:26:03,060 --> 00:26:05,560
The result was drastically escalating unemployment.

349
00:26:06,520 --> 00:26:12,720
By 1932, 15 million Americans, or 30% of the country's workforce, were out of work.

350
00:26:13,860 --> 00:26:16,000
Bank failures fed off that vicious cycle.

351
00:26:16,740 --> 00:26:22,100
I would say, in some sense, the Great Depression actually hit rural areas of America before the 1930s.

352
00:26:22,101 --> 00:26:25,600
low crop prices, farmers couldn't repay their bank loans.

353
00:26:25,780 --> 00:26:28,540
But these were little tiny banks in places like North Dakota,

354
00:26:28,800 --> 00:26:31,320
and they didn't seem to have much impact on the rest of the country.

355
00:26:31,780 --> 00:26:35,300
What happened in the 30s is that as prices fell,

356
00:26:35,560 --> 00:26:38,520
lots of businesses could not repay their bank loans.

357
00:26:38,920 --> 00:26:40,740
Therefore, the banks closed their doors.

358
00:26:41,280 --> 00:26:43,900
Therefore, the public lost their money.

359
00:26:44,560 --> 00:26:47,200
And the idea grew that you ought to just get cash

360
00:26:47,201 --> 00:26:49,080
and keep it under your pillow or in your mattress.

361
00:26:49,081 --> 00:26:58,020
I can assure you that it is safer for you to keep your money in a reopened bank than to keep it under the mattress.

362
00:26:59,700 --> 00:27:08,162
President Roosevelt's response to the bank failures was the New Deal legislation that included banking reforms that would change the industry for more than half a

363
00:27:08,163 --> 00:27:08,863
century.

364
00:27:09,660 --> 00:27:13,100
Among its provisions, a new concept called deposit insurance.

365
00:27:13,101 --> 00:27:18,680
The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation guarantees each account up to $100,000.

366
00:27:19,080 --> 00:27:21,120
That's an important element of confidence.

367
00:27:21,560 --> 00:27:24,620
It doesn't mean much to a business that might leave millions of dollars

368
00:27:24,621 --> 00:27:26,880
or transfer millions of dollars through a system,

369
00:27:27,420 --> 00:27:32,160
but that insurance gives the individuals confidence that their money is safe and sound.

370
00:27:33,120 --> 00:27:38,320
The government also took drastic action that split the banking industry into separate parts.

371
00:27:39,220 --> 00:27:43,640
It was decided that because of the stock market crash and the depression,

372
00:27:43,641 --> 00:27:49,020
that it would be a good idea to break off commercial banking from investment banking.

373
00:27:49,080 --> 00:27:52,060
Commercial banking deals with loans and deposits.

374
00:27:52,260 --> 00:27:56,120
Investment banking deals with underwriting securities, issuing new securities.

375
00:27:56,680 --> 00:28:01,340
The Glass-Steagall Act in 1933 decided that bankers would have to choose

376
00:28:01,341 --> 00:28:05,620
either to be commercial bankers or investment bankers, but they couldn't be both.

377
00:28:06,960 --> 00:28:09,680
It was thought that banks would be less likely to fail

378
00:28:09,681 --> 00:28:12,540
if they were not operating as financial supermarkets.

379
00:28:13,660 --> 00:28:17,800
Economists today believe that bigger financial institutions are much safer

380
00:28:17,801 --> 00:28:20,060
because their risks are diversified.

381
00:28:20,840 --> 00:28:23,260
The merger between Citibank and Travelers Insurance

382
00:28:23,261 --> 00:28:25,800
that created the financial behemoth of Citigroup

383
00:28:25,801 --> 00:28:28,520
would have been illegal had the Glass-Steagall Act

384
00:28:28,521 --> 00:28:30,720
not been repealed in 1999.

385
00:28:32,860 --> 00:28:37,000
Coming up next, the evolution of bank security systems.

386
00:28:37,520 --> 00:28:40,100
If all the currency in the five-story vault

387
00:28:40,101 --> 00:28:42,040
of the Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas

388
00:28:42,041 --> 00:28:43,740
was stacked in a single pile,

389
00:28:44,420 --> 00:28:46,760
it would reach 17 miles high.

390
00:28:47,800 --> 00:28:50,980
Banks will continue on Modern Marvels.

391
00:28:53,120 --> 00:28:56,940
We now return to Banks on Modern Marvels.

392
00:29:00,780 --> 00:29:07,240
As long as there have been banks, there have been criminals who have been attracted to their cash assets like insects attracted to light.

393
00:29:07,880 --> 00:29:11,620
That was never more true than in the Wild West of the late 1800s,

394
00:29:11,920 --> 00:29:16,960
when marauding bands of outlaws terrorized banks in small towns on the American frontier.

395
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:22,800
Perhaps the most famous outlaw of the era was Jesse James.

396
00:29:22,801 --> 00:29:25,800
His most notorious holdup took place in 1876,

397
00:29:25,801 --> 00:29:29,800
when he and his gang tried to rob a bank in Minnesota.

398
00:29:29,801 --> 00:29:31,800
When the bank clerk refused to open the safe,

399
00:29:31,801 --> 00:29:35,800
James and his accomplices murdered him in cold blood.

400
00:29:35,801 --> 00:29:38,800
In the shootout that followed, all of the outlaws except Jesse James

401
00:29:38,801 --> 00:29:42,800
and his older brother Frank were either killed or captured.

402
00:29:42,801 --> 00:29:49,560
Fifty years later, the Great Depression triggered another nationwide spree of bank robberies

403
00:29:49,561 --> 00:29:53,840
and elevated a new generation of criminals to legendary status.

404
00:29:53,841 --> 00:29:59,300
The FBI named one of them, John Dillinger, public enemy number one for his daring bank

405
00:29:59,301 --> 00:30:00,300
robberies.

406
00:30:00,301 --> 00:30:06,140
Dillinger's gang included men with nicknames that belied their violent natures.

407
00:30:06,141 --> 00:30:11,320
Babyface Nelson and Pretty Boy Floyd, both of whom were later killed in gun battles with

408
00:30:11,321 --> 00:30:12,021
police.

409
00:30:12,022 --> 00:30:17,860
It is at the very core of a bank's promise to its customers that the bank will

410
00:30:17,861 --> 00:30:23,040
keep deposits safe and sound from both economic peril and human greed.

411
00:30:23,041 --> 00:30:24,520
In the old days, the

412
00:30:24,521 --> 00:30:30,020
thought was to separate the teller from the bad guys, if you will, and you had that caged

413
00:30:30,021 --> 00:30:30,783
environment.

414
00:30:30,784 --> 00:30:35,740
Cages were meant to protect the teller from the threat of harm while providing

415
00:30:35,741 --> 00:30:37,740
a possible deterrent against bank robbery.

416
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:40,180
For the most part, they worked.

417
00:30:40,880 --> 00:30:43,400
Tellers in their cash drawers were kept at a safe distance

418
00:30:43,401 --> 00:30:45,500
from the threat of intrusion from a bank robber.

419
00:30:46,840 --> 00:30:50,340
Primitive bank alarm systems were developed in the late 1800s.

420
00:30:51,160 --> 00:30:54,740
They started with what they called a multi-box alarm

421
00:30:54,741 --> 00:30:56,760
in buildings that they were monitoring,

422
00:30:57,000 --> 00:30:59,740
and by just pushing a button in those locations,

423
00:31:00,220 --> 00:31:03,580
it would send a certain kind of telegraph message to the office.

424
00:31:03,980 --> 00:31:05,340
Later, there was a tripwire.

425
00:31:05,740 --> 00:31:12,920
And if somebody would walk into the building after hours and trip the wire, it actually sent one of those signals to the central station.

426
00:31:12,921 --> 00:31:17,720
So hence the term tripping an alarm, which is still what's used today.

427
00:31:19,340 --> 00:31:26,080
A modern bank typically is protected by a myriad of alarm systems that can be activated both manually and automatically.

428
00:31:27,780 --> 00:31:31,900
A bill trap is actually a device that is placed in the teller drawer.

429
00:31:31,901 --> 00:31:36,700
and without getting too specific and giving away any of the industry secrets,

430
00:31:36,920 --> 00:31:39,720
it's a device that is part of the robbery alarm system.

431
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:42,680
By a teller just simply taking the money out of the drawer,

432
00:31:43,100 --> 00:31:46,780
there's a way that it is activated and, in fact, it sends an alarm signal.

433
00:31:49,240 --> 00:31:53,600
Bullet-resistant glass was developed that would help shield bank tellers from gunmen

434
00:31:53,601 --> 00:31:55,360
who would open fire during a holdup.

435
00:31:58,360 --> 00:32:01,520
Technology allowed the glass to get thinner but stronger.

436
00:32:01,900 --> 00:32:05,800
Bullet-resistant glass is mainly used at a bank's drive-up window.

437
00:32:06,540 --> 00:32:10,580
However, sophisticated alarm systems remain at the core of a bank's security.

438
00:32:11,880 --> 00:32:19,140
Burglar alarm system for attacks that would take place in an intrusion-type alarm after hours when nobody's in the bank.

439
00:32:19,480 --> 00:32:24,980
And then, of course, there's a robbery system that would be used to notify law enforcement in the event that a bank robbery takes place.

440
00:32:24,981 --> 00:32:28,920
We began to see the use of 35-millimeter or hard-print cameras.

441
00:32:28,921 --> 00:32:34,320
They were normally put over the doors to catch the full face of someone as they were leaving the office.

442
00:32:34,700 --> 00:32:43,140
We're seeing the use of closed-circuit television as a much greater, quick way to get photographs of someone who's been in the bank.

443
00:32:43,360 --> 00:32:48,160
Today, within minutes, you can have photographs of people out of those closed-circuit television systems.

444
00:32:48,480 --> 00:32:55,200
We're seeing digital recording, which allows a very quick instant use and email of photographs to a police car.

445
00:32:55,201 --> 00:33:02,160
To detect bank burglaries after hours, bank lobbies are crisscrossed by a network of sensors.

446
00:33:02,600 --> 00:33:04,780
Infrared is a form of motion detector.

447
00:33:04,980 --> 00:33:08,620
Infrared measures body heat or measures a heat, ambient heat temperature.

448
00:33:08,940 --> 00:33:13,140
Once an area is empty or vacant, a certain ambient temperature gets established.

449
00:33:13,680 --> 00:33:17,400
If a human being who has a certain body temperature walks into that,

450
00:33:17,480 --> 00:33:20,660
it causes a spike in that temperature and sets an alarm off.

451
00:33:20,661 --> 00:33:22,000
There's also ultrasound.

452
00:33:22,001 --> 00:33:28,180
sound waves are actually transmitted and it's the breaking of those waves and intrusion into

453
00:33:28,181 --> 00:33:33,300
the pattern of waves that takes place that again causes an event to be reacted to.

454
00:33:34,780 --> 00:33:40,480
When ultrasound and infrared devices are used in tandem, both must trip independently in order

455
00:33:40,481 --> 00:33:41,608
to send an alarm.

456
00:33:41,609 --> 00:33:46,320
The system is going to be controlled through a control box where all of

457
00:33:46,321 --> 00:33:50,080
the various devices that can trip the alarm feed into.

458
00:33:50,200 --> 00:33:52,740
So you have a centralized point in the office

459
00:33:52,741 --> 00:33:55,400
that is kind of the brains of the alarm system.

460
00:33:56,620 --> 00:33:59,120
The simplest system involves a dialer

461
00:33:59,121 --> 00:34:01,560
and sends the alarm out over a telephone line,

462
00:34:01,561 --> 00:34:03,140
